Dolphin (https://github.com/libretro/dolphin, the libretro front of https://github.com/dolphin-emu/dolphin,
GPL-2.0-or-later, Copyright the Dolphin Emulator Project) runs the GameCube. The tools apply this change of Dreamports'
own to its source before building it on the contributor's computer (tools/consoles.mjs, tools/libretro/build.mjs), so
that its software renderer, which draws on the processor in whole numbers, the same picture on every computer, runs
with no window and no graphics card, and so that the sound the tools hear is the console's own samples.

As its makers publish it, the libretro front starts each renderer when the graphics context it asked whoever runs it
for arrives. The software renderer asks for none, so nothing starts it, and when it is started it still wants an OpenGL
context, only to put its finished picture in a window. With this change the front starts the software renderer itself,
before the console boots, as the others are; the renderer makes no OpenGL context under libretro; and each picture it
shows is handed to whoever runs the core as it is in memory, the stretch of it that's shown, with a pixel's colours in
the order libretro's XRGB8888 has them, where the renderer's texture has red first.

Dolphin's mixer makes the computer's sound from the console's, at another rate, in floats, stretched as the computer's
speed asks. With this change the samples the console's audio interface takes from its memory by DMA are handed on as
they are, 8 at a time, at the interface's own rate, which whoever runs the core is told each time a game changes it.
Sound a game streams from its disc isn't among them yet.

Two settings Dolphin takes from the computer are fixed, so the same copy plays the same everywhere: a program given
alone, or a disc with no region of its own, runs as an American console's, where Dolphin would take the computer's
country, which sets the video interface's rate as the console starts; and the console's clock starts at the first of
January 2002 and moves with the emulated time, as it does for one of Dolphin's movies, where it would follow the
computer's own clock.

It also has the front look for no real controller on the computer, a GameCube controller adapter over USB or a Wii
Remote over Bluetooth: a run's buttons are the ones the tools give it and nothing else, and a search for a Wii Remote
kept the program from ending on Windows.

Nothing of the console changes: the processor, the video interface, the renderer's drawing and the audio interface are
as Dolphin has them. Nothing of Dolphin is in the repository but the lines of its own this change sits between.

